    <!-- 06 UGC as the Dominant Format -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="ugc">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">06 &mdash; UGC Format</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">UGC is the dominant format for performance creative in 2026</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">UGC-style ads outperform polished brand ads on CPA across most categories</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Platform algorithms favor native-looking content in feeds</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">UGC production is faster, cheaper, and more modular than studio production</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The data is clear: across most DTC, SaaS, and consumer tech categories in 2026, UGC-style video ads outperform polished brand ads on CPA. The reasons are structural, not aesthetic.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Trust.</strong> A real person talking about a product is more credible than a brand talking about itself. Viewers have learned to skip anything that looks like an ad. UGC doesn't look like an ad &mdash; it looks like content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Algorithm preference.</strong> Meta, TikTok, and YouTube algorithms serve content that looks native to the feed. UGC matches the visual language of organic content. Polished brand ads trigger "ad blindness" and get lower initial distribution.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Production speed.</strong> A UGC creator can produce a finished ad in hours. A studio production takes weeks. When you need 20&ndash;50 new ads per month, UGC is the only production model that scales.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For deeper context on UGC for ads, see our <a href="/ugc-ads" class="text-accent hover:underline">UGC ads guide</a>. For <a href="/tech-ugc" class="text-accent hover:underline">Tech UGC</a> specifically, the education-first approach applies the same principles to software and app products.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07 Modular Production -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="modular">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; Modular</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Modular production: shoot components, remix into dozens of variations</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">6 hooks &times; 4 bodies &times; 3 CTAs = 72 unique ads from 13 components</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Modular production cuts cost 80%+ vs shooting individual ads</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Test at the component level: find winning hooks, then winning bodies, then CTAs</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Modular production is the backbone of performance creative at scale. Instead of shooting 72 individual ads, you shoot 13 components (6 hooks, 4 body narratives, 3 CTAs) and combine them in the edit. The math: 6 &times; 4 &times; 3 = 72 unique ad variations from a single production session.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">For a complete breakdown of the <a href="/modular-creative" class="text-accent hover:underline">modular creative</a> approach &mdash; including production workflow, naming conventions, component analytics, and scaling strategy &mdash; see our dedicated guide.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Production workflow</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Script modular concepts.</strong> Write scripts where hooks, bodies, and CTAs are designed as separate, interchangeable components.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Shoot all hooks in one session.</strong> Same creator, same setup, different openings. Maximize hook variants &mdash; these are the highest-leverage component.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Shoot bodies and CTAs separately.</strong> Product demos, testimonials, problem/solution narratives, and CTA closings in dedicated sessions.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Edit into combinations.</strong> Assemble hook + body + CTA combinations. Name each variation systematically: [concept]_[hook-variant]_[body-variant]_[cta-variant] for tracking.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Why modular beats traditional</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Traditional ad production creates finished, monolithic ads. If the hook doesn't work, the entire ad is wasted. Modular production lets you swap the hook while keeping the body &mdash; recycling what works and replacing what doesn't. This dramatically increases the hit rate and reduces the cost per winning ad.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Data-Driven Iteration -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="iteration">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Iteration</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Data-driven iteration: CPA-based decisions, not opinions</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">CPA is the decision metric &mdash; everything else is supporting data</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Scale winners, kill losers within 72h, iterate near-misses</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Winning ads are iterated versions of near-misses, not first-try home runs</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">In performance creative, the creative director is the data. A beautiful ad with high CPA gets killed. An "ugly" ad with low CPA gets scaled. This discipline is what separates performance creative from brand creative &mdash; and what makes it uncomfortable for teams used to judging creative by how it looks.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The decision framework</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Decision</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">CPA Signal</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Action</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #2e7d32">Scale</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">CPA &gt;20% below target</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Increase budget 20&ndash;30% every 3 days, produce variations</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #f57f17">Iterate</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">CPA within &plusmn;20% of target</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Change one variable (hook, angle, creator), retest</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #c62828">Kill</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">CPA &gt;50% above target</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Stop spend immediately, analyze why, apply learnings</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Diagnosing with hook rate</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">CPA tells you <em>what</em> happened. <a href="/hook-rate" class="text-accent hover:underline">Hook rate</a> tells you <em>why</em>. If hook rate is bad and CPA is bad, the problem is the opening &mdash; the ad isn't stopping the scroll. If hook rate is good but CPA is bad, the problem is the body or offer &mdash; people are watching but not converting. This diagnostic framework prevents the most common mistake: changing the hook when the real problem is the body.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Creative fatigue and replacement</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Every winning ad eventually fatigues. On Meta in 2026, performance typically starts declining at weekly frequency &gt;2.5 and crashes past 4.0. The question is not whether fatigue happens, but how fast you replace fatigued creatives. A continuous performance creative system ensures new winners are always in the pipeline. For more on identifying and managing fatigue, see our <a href="/ad-fatigue" class="text-accent hover:underline">ad fatigue guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

